Course Objective:
After completing this course, you are be capable of performing the following tasks:

Pick an appropriate (parametric) statistical model for data collected via random sampling;

Use the Method of Moments, Maximum Likelihood Principle, and Bayes’ Theorem to construct estimators;

Compute the Mean Squared Error (MSE) of an estimator and express it in terms of the bias and variance of the estimator;

Construct confidence intervals for unknown model parameters;

Understand how sample size influences confidence intervals;

Formalise questions about a proportion or a parameter as statistical hypotheses;

Carry out a statistical test with pre-specified significance level both in one sample and two sample settings;

Understand the relation between sample size and the power of a test;

Use simple linear regression to model relations between responses and covariates;

Use the least squares approach, construct confidence intervals, and perform hypothesis tests in the simple linear regression model.

Use statistical software to carry out the above tasks.

Course Content:
This course is an introduction to fundamental concepts and methods in Mathematical Statistics. In this course we work with parametric models – this means that we know the distribution of the data up to some unknown parameters. We collect a random sample from such a model and want to make informed, quantifiable statements about those parameters. Our main focus is on modelling, point estimation, uncertainty quantification, and hypothesis testing. We also cover the simple linear regression model.

Teaching Methods:
Lectures, exercise classes

Method of Assessment:
Two assignments covering computation and theory (up to 10% of final grade) and written examination/resit examination (at least 90% of final grade). There is no resit possibility for the assignments. In addition to a weighted average of at least 5.5, to pass the course, the exam grade (or the resit grade) needs to be at least 5.5.

In the exams, no gen-AI use is allowed (i.e., level 1 of gen-AI use). In the take-home assignments, gen-AI is allowed but you must declare in the hand-in that AI was used (i.e., level 3 of gen-AI use).

